A||N||4||fierovette||A/C retrofit, anyone try it||||5||
Z||000000||fierovette||06-30-2001||08:34 AM||||My A/C freezes up. I was told at the OOB show that I hav air in the system.??? Also I may as well retrofit to R-134a. Has anyone tried it? Any problems? Kit on sale at K-mart, $29.96. Figured I give it a try. How many cans of R-134a are needed? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodrv6||06-30-2001||09:03 AM||||Do a search--this has been covered extensivly recently (must have something to do with summer heat <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ).  I did a 134 conversion on my 84 about 3 years ago and am very pleased.  I think it took 2 cans.  Like everything else, there is a right and wrong way to do it--check out some recent threads.<P>And welcome to the forum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000002||cadaver||07-04-2001||02:39 AM||||If it freezes it has moisture in it not air. What you should do is have it recovered then evacuated the recharged with you old stuff.<BR>Everyone wants to go to r134 when there is no need to.||||reg||1
Z||000003||LarryB||07-04-2001||01:04 PM||||There's not need to convert to r134A, but needing 2.5 lbs of R12 @ $50-70/lb before adding in parts or labor is a pretty strong incentive to do so.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadaver:<BR><B>If it freezes it has moisture in it not air. What you should do is have it recovered then evacuated the recharged with you old stuff.<BR>Everyone wants to go to r134 when there is no need to.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||MontanaFiero||07-04-2001||05:40 PM||||I ordered and will be using <B>AutoFrost</B>, which is R406A refrigerant. It is a substitute for R12 and can use the same oil as R12. But it has to be done right.  Some will say don't do it, because there are many alternative refrigerants out there, and several of them have compatability issues. Also, good luck finding a local A/C shop that is willing to work with refrigerants they don't know about or understand.  But I say use it anyway. The data sheets I have looked through prove that R406A returns the oil back to the compressor better than R134A and is also cooler.  This "recharge" costs less than $200 when you do it right (replace the dryer, orifice tube and all the seals on a car this old) the recharge kit itself is only $50 with two cans of autofrost.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.autofrost.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autofrost.com</A> <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg"> ||||reg||1
